.TH TIME 3C "August 1, 1992" 
.UC 4
.SH NAME
time \- get system time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <time.h>
.PP
.B time_t time(time_t *\fItloc\fR);
.fi
.SH "(NOT AVAILABLE IN POSIX)"
.nf
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.B #include <sys/timeb.h>
.PP
.B ftime(struct timeb *\fItp\fP);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.ft B
For non-POSIX applications, these interfaces are obsoleted by gettimeofday(2).
.ft R
.PP
.I Time
returns the value of time in seconds since 00:00:00 GMT, January 1, 
1970.
.PP
If
.I tloc
is nonnull,
the return value is also stored in the
place to which
.I tloc
points.
.PP
The BSD
.I ftime
function fills in a structure pointed to by its argument,
as defined in
.RI < sys/timeb.h >.
The structure contains the time
since the epoch in seconds,
up to 1000 milliseconds of more-precise interval,
the local time zone
(measured in minutes of time westward from Greenwich),
and a flag that, if nonzero, indicates that
Daylight Saving time applies locally
during the appropriate part of the year.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion, 
.I time
returns the value of time.  Otherwise, a value of ((
.I time_t
)\-1) is
returned.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
date(1), gettimeofday(2), settimeofday(2), ctime(3)
